NAME
       grub2-set-default - choose the GRUB menu entry that boots by default

SYNOPSIS
       grub2-set-default MENU_ENTRY

DESCRIPTION
       grub2-set-default stores MENU_ENTRY as saved_entry in the GRUB environment
       block (/boot/grub2/grubenv). From the next boot on GRUB starts that entry
       when nobody picks another one. MENU_ENTRY is either a number (0 is the
       first entry of the menu, normally the newest kernel) or the id of an
       entry, which on RHEL 9 is the name of its file in /boot/loader/entries
       without ".conf".

       This works because RHEL 9 sets GRUB_DEFAULT=saved in /etc/default/grub.
       grubby --set-default does the same job and accepts a kernel path, which
       is usually easier.

EXAMPLES
       Boot the second entry (often the previous kernel) from now on:
              # grub2-set-default 1
              # grub2-editenv list | grep saved_entry

FILES
       /boot/grub2/grubenv, /boot/loader/entries/*.conf

SEE ALSO
       grubby(8), grub2-editenv(1), grub2-mkconfig(8)
